\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We evaluate on four English NER benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient.
\textbf{SciERC} \cite{luan2018scierc} (551 test instances, 6 entity types) is the primary medium-difficulty test bed (${\approx}$65\% F1).
\textbf{Few-NERD} \cite{ding2021fewnerd} (INTRA coarse; 37{,}648 instances, 8 entity types, ${\approx}$75\% F1) provides a low-degeneracy contrast where LP selection succeeds (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).
\textbf{CoNLL-2003} \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} (3{,}453 test sentences, 4 entity types, ${\approx}$91\% F1) serves as a ceiling-effect control.
\textbf{WNUT-17} \cite{derczynski2017wnut} (1{,}287 social media instances, 53.7\% zero F1) probes the signal-collapse boundary.

\paragraph{Models and sampling.}
\textbf{Qwen3-8B} and \textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct} are LoRA fine-tuned using LLaMA Factory \cite{zheng2024llamafactory} (Appendix~\ref{app:training}; Few-NERD uses 3 epochs vs.\ 5 for others; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).
$N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ (primary) with vLLM constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}; $N {\in} \{16, 32, 64\}$ for scaling (Figure~\ref{fig:n_scaling}; Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_n_scaling_mv.pdf}
\caption{MV $\Delta$F1 vs.\ $\log_2 N$ across three datasets (Qwen3-8B FT, seed${=}42$). Dotted gray: $\Delta{=}0$. Gains are dataset-dependent: persistently negative on Few-NERD, near zero on SciERC at higher $N$, marginally positive on CoNLL ($+0.6$\,pp at $N{=}64$).}
\label{fig:n_scaling}
\end{figure}

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Quality Estimation Results}\label{sec:qe_results}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{NER quality estimation ($\rho$ and AUROC; $N{=}8$, primary seed${=}42$; 3-seed $\sigma{\leq}0.026$ in Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}). AUROC binarizes at per-dataset median F1. Best in \textbf{bold}. RE in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}; $N{=}16$ in Table~\ref{tab:robustness}.}
\label{tab:quality_estimation}
\begin{tabular}{ll cc cc cc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (Qwen)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (LLaMA)}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6} \cmidrule(lr){7-8}
Signal & Family & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC \\
\midrule
VC & Structural & \textbf{.379} & .684 & .424 & .789 & .426 & .709 \\
SJ  & Structural & .360 & .692 & .436 & .801 & \textbf{.431} & .704 \\
FK & Surface & .267 & .629 & .400 & .774 & .428 & .703 \\
EM  & Surface    & .295 & \textbf{.749} & \textbf{.451} & \textbf{.816} & \textbf{.431} & .705 \\
\midrule
LP     & Model      & .205 & .578 & .316 & .753 & .307 & \textbf{.761} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation} presents $\rho$ and AUROC for five signals across tasks and models.

\paragraph{Signal rankings are difficulty-dependent.}
On SciERC, structural signals lead (VC $\rho{=}0.379$, SJ $0.360$) over LP ($0.205$); bootstrap CIs confirm the gap ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.151{\pm}0.006$, 5 seeds; Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}).
On CoNLL (F1${\approx}$0.91), EM leads both $\rho$ and AUROC ($0.816$).
At the signal-collapse boundary (WNUT-17, F1${\approx}$42\%), full-set correlations are dominated by the zero/non-zero boundary (VC $\rho{=}0.724$ at $N{=}16$, but conditional $\rho{=}0.038$; Table~\ref{tab:wnut}); LP shows the reverse (conditional $\rho{=}0.350$; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Cross-Dataset Contrast: Few-NERD}\label{sec:fewnerd}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{LP selection across datasets (instance-averaged F1; Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, gold-filtered, 3-seed means). $p$: paired bootstrap.}
\label{tab:fewnerd}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccccr@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & $n$ & Greedy & Degen.\% & LP $\rho$ & LP $\Delta$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
WNUT-17 & 1{,}287 & .452 & 67.9\% & $-$.446 & $-$0.45\,pp & .72 \\
CoNLL & 2{,}756 & .908 & 61.7\% & .302 & $-$0.85\,pp & 1.0 \\
SciERC & 529 & .663 & 12.2\% & .159 & $+$0.61\,pp & .193 \\
Few-NERD & 32{,}565 & .748 & 13.0\% & .220 & $\mathbf{+1.39}$\,pp & ${<}.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}) confirms LP selection at low degeneracy ($+1.39$~pp, $p{<}0.001$; structural signals degrade: VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$~pp), with LP 50$\times$ more seed-stable ($\sigma{=}0.04$ vs.\ SJ $2.02$~pp), generalizing to LLaMA (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}). Training convergence modulates degeneracy (5-epoch: $31$\%, LP $\Delta$ collapses from $+1.39$ to $+0.09$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).

Signal ranking is task-specific; per-type analysis shows MV degradation concentrates in low-F1 types ($\rho{=}0.90$, $p{=}0.002$; Appendix~\ref{app:pertype}).

\paragraph{Degeneracy alone does not predict LP direction.}
Degeneracy does not predict LP gain across 23 configurations ($\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$; $n{=}23$, insufficient for reliable correlation---we treat this as supplementary evidence; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), though it co-varies with LP within fixed settings ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_degeneracy_lp_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Instance degeneracy vs.\ LP selection gain across 23 configurations ($N{=}8$; $\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$). Within Few-NERD per-entity-type ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}), degeneracy predicts LP gain when alignment is held constant.}
\label{fig:degeneracy_scatter}
\end{figure}

MV gains range from $-2.71$\,pp (Few-NERD) to $+0.6$\,pp (CoNLL at $N{=}64$; Figure~\ref{fig:n_scaling}); the optimal strategy is dataset-dependent (LP-best $+1.4$\,pp on Few-NERD vs.\ MV $+0.61$\,pp on CoNLL).
The framework extends to RE, where $40$\% zero-F1 from upstream entity span errors creates a distinct failure mode---\textit{diversity among failures} rather than quality-differentiated outputs---despite comparable degeneracy ($10.1$\% vs.\ SciERC $13.4$\%; Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}\label{sec:robustness_main}

Signal rankings are robust across temperatures, seeds, and $N$ (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).
Qwen3-4B QE matches 8B ($|\Delta\rho|{=}0.042$); LoRA rank${=}8$ lowers degeneracy but LP stays below greedy (Appendix~\ref{app:supervised_combination}).
The gap persists from 4B to 72B zero-shot to GPT-5.5 (\S\ref{sec:scaling}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Prescriptive Results}\label{sec:prescriptive}

Given that sample-level selection fails uniformly (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}), we ask whether entity-level aggregation can extract value from multi-sample generation under favorable diagnostic conditions.

\paragraph{Entity-level construction (exploratory).}
We aggregate entities across samples, retaining those exceeding $\theta{=}2/N$, with LP-weighted or uniform scoring ($\theta$ stable in $[0.15, 0.35]$; Appendix~\ref{app:theta_sweep}).
The diagnostic framework predicts gains when degeneracy and LP alignment are both favorable.
LP-weighted construction yields $+1.40{\pm}0.51$\,pp on Few-NERD ($p{<}0.001$; Table~\ref{tab:entity_construction}), consistent with this prediction ($D{=}13$\%, headroom${=}12.4$\,pp), a smaller non-significant $+0.32$\,pp on SciERC, and no gain on CoNLL ($-0.39$\,pp).\footnote{Degenerate instances contribute $\Delta{=}0$ by construction; gains concentrate in the non-degenerate subset ($+3.36$\,pp SciERC, $+5.18$\,pp CoNLL, $+7.08$\,pp Few-NERD).}
Under global Holm--Bonferroni \cite{holm1979simple} correction across all $k{=}57$ comparisons (5 seeds), $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.013$---below $\alpha{=}0.05$, providing statistically significant evidence of construction gains under the most conservative correction.
Per-family correction ($k{=}10$): $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.006$; BH FDR: $6/10$ construction comparisons at FDR${<}0.05$ (Appendix~\ref{app:holm}).
The family partition was defined by mechanism type but not pre-registered; we report all three corrections for transparency.
Construction and LP selection are complementary ($\rho_{\text{adj}}$: Few-NERD $0.516$); QE signals support selective prediction ($+5$--$8$\,pp at 80\% coverage; Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).

\paragraph{Epoch confound on Few-NERD.}
Few-NERD positive results should be interpreted cautiously: the 3-epoch model may be under-converged, and 5-epoch training eliminates LP gains entirely ($+1.39{\to}+0.09{\pm}0.04$\,pp, 94\% collapse; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).
Entity construction likewise degrades: LP-weighted $\Delta$ drops from $+0.88$ to $+0.20$\,pp (seed 42, macro-F1; $77$\% reduction).
The diagnostic framework correctly predicts this: 5-epoch constant-F1 degeneracy rises to $31$\% (vs.\ $13$\% at 3 epochs; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}), degrading the conditions for both selection and construction.\footnote{Degeneracy rates vary by definition: constant-F1 degeneracy (all $N$ samples yield identical F1) gives $31$\%/$13$\%; entity-set exact-match degeneracy gives $26$\%/$9$\%. The directional pattern is consistent.}
This sensitivity---favorable conditions are fragile and training-dependent---reinforces that Few-NERD is a conditional success rather than evidence of a general bypass. The core negative finding (selection ${\leq}$ greedy under high degeneracy) is unaffected.

\phantomsection\label{sec:selection_results}

\section{Where Single-Signal Selection Fails}\label{sec:selection_gap}

\paragraph{Definitional vs.\ empirical.}
We decompose the correlation-selection gap into two layers.
The first is structural: four of five signals are instance-level aggregates that assign identical scores to all $N$ candidates from a given input (\S\ref{sec:formulation}), so they estimate quality across inputs but cannot rank candidates within an instance---selection failure is a design property, not an empirical finding.
The second layer is empirical and constitutes the core diagnostic: LP---the only per-sample signal---suffers compression under constrained decoding, with 60\% of SciERC instances showing within-instance LP range below 0.041 nats (Appendix~\ref{app:logprob_ties}), effectively eliminating the variation needed for sample discrimination. This compression is model-intrinsic: free-form decoding yields nearly identical LP distributions (within-instance std ratio $0.97{\times}$; Appendix~\ref{app:freeform_ablation}). Even where LP varies, selection gains depend on LP-quality alignment rather than degeneracy alone.
Cross-instance $\rho$ (Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation}) is attenuated by varying instance difficulty; within-instance $\rho$ directly measures whether LP discriminates candidates for a fixed input, making it the relevant predictor of selection success.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Best-of-$N$ selection (macro F1) on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Selection signals: 2-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$; baselines: 3-seed (Oracle${=}0.781$, Greedy${=}0.667$). LP is per-sample; others use consensus-proximity. $^\dagger$One score per input.}
\label{tab:selection}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
Method & NER F1 & $p$ \\
\midrule
Oracle & $0.775 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
Greedy ($T{=}0$) & $0.645 \pm 0.001$ & --- \\
\midrule
LP           & $\mathbf{0.647} \pm 0.005$ & .29 \\
SJ           & $0.646 \pm 0.002$ & .67 \\
FK           & $0.646 \pm 0.001$ & .79 \\
EM           & $0.637 \pm 0.001$ & n/a$^\dagger$ \\
VC           & $0.623 \pm 0.004$ & n/a$^\dagger$ \\
\midrule
Random       & $0.610 \pm 0.003$ & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{fig_selection_gap}
\caption{Correlation-selection gap on SciERC ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Bars: QE $\rho$; line: $\Delta$F1 vs.\ greedy (3-seed mean). Despite $\rho$ up to 0.38, no signal outperforms greedy (all $p{\geq}0.29$). Dashed green: oracle headroom ($+11.4$\,pp; oracle headroom $=$ best-of-$N$ F1 $-$ greedy F1); dotted gray: random.}
\label{fig:selection_gap}
\end{figure}

Despite $\rho$ up to 0.38, no signal outperforms greedy on SciERC (Table~\ref{tab:selection}; Figure~\ref{fig:selection_gap}); the oracle shows 11.4\,pp headroom, but LP achieves $+0.2$\,pp ($p{=}0.29$), below MDE (${\approx}$1.5\,pp; $n{=}529$; Table~\ref{tab:power_analysis}).
The pattern holds across CoNLL and WNUT-17 (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).
Few-NERD provides the definitive contrast: LP achieves $+1.39$\,pp ($p{<}0.001$; Holm $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.021$, selection family $k{=}21$)\footnote{Under global correction ($k{=}57$, 5 seeds), LP-best selection reaches Holm $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.013$ (BH $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.003$)---surviving Holm--Bonferroni at $\alpha{=}0.05$.} at low degeneracy (13.0\% vs.\ 12.2--67.9\%), while structural signals still degrade (VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$\,pp; \S\ref{sec:fewnerd}).

\paragraph{Diagnostic framework.}
We identify two co-occurring factors that jointly characterize the gap:
(1)~instance degeneracy sets the ceiling (SciERC 12.2\%, CoNLL 61.7\%);
(2)~oracle headroom bounds the opportunity within non-degenerate instances (CoNLL ${\approx}4$\,pp vs.\ SciERC 11.4\,pp);
(3)~LP compression limits exploitation (Few-NERD within-instance $\rho{\approx}0.5$--$0.7$ vs.\ SciERC $\rho{\approx}0.26$).
Degeneracy does not predict LP gain across datasets ($\rho{=}0.052$, $n{=}23$, $p{=}0.81$; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), though it co-varies with LP within fixed settings ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).
These conditions interact ($R^2{<}0$); on non-degenerate instances, $\alpha$ approaches zero ($0.000$--$0.161$) despite $\rho$ up to 0.45 (\S\ref{sec:theory}).

\paragraph{Mechanistic evidence (Appendix~\ref{sec:mechanistic}).}
Why does signal alignment collapse? Probing experiments on Qwen2.5-7B\footnote{Probes use Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct (not the fine-tuned Qwen3-8B); Qwen3-8B's thinking mode may alter self-evaluation dynamics. Conclusions are architecture-specific.} reveal an \textit{introspection boundary}: hidden-state linear probes achieve entity-level AUROC $0.63$--$0.88$, substantially exceeding prompt-based self-judgment (ESJ AUROC $0.51$, indistinguishable from chance), yet cross-dataset transfer drops to $0.56$--$0.67$ (LODO).
Entity self-judgment, multi-round refinement ($-3.1$\,pp vs.\ greedy), and latent centroid selection ($-3.5$\,pp) all fail to improve over greedy decoding.
Entity quality is partially encoded in representations but inaccessible through the generation interface---consistent with the signal alignment collapse observed across all configurations.

